A||||12||montage||Warning: 88 Owners Rear Tie Rob Mount||||1||
Z||000000||montage||07-23-2001||08:40 AM||||I just finished rebuilding the front end with new rear struts and tires. After alignment we noticed a severe torque steer like affect under acceleration (I can't believe they missed this during alignment but they agreed to realign for free). I had noticed this before but tires and a bad strut masked this. Upon further inspection I discovered a broken rear tire passenger tie rod mount on the cradle. There was no corrosion and this looked to be a fatigue fracture. The mount is a U channel with the last ~ 2" of the top open. The front side of the mount is a hole for the tie rod bushing bolt. The rear has a much larger hole and it appears as though a large washer is press fit into this hole with the washer being fastened by a nut to the tie rod bushing. The hole is very close to the edge of the mount (at least on mine) and this hole ripped open causing the bushing to be pulled out hanging on by the other end of the bolt. Upon inspection of the driver side mount I noticed a crack starting to form on the rear side of the cradle mount. There was about a 1/16 separation between the washer and the large hole at about the 2 o'clock position. I tried a search with on luck. This could be isolated but I would think everyone with an 88 should at least inspect these mounts. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||88CoupeV6||07-23-2001||09:09 AM||||I will check both of my 88's today. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||7
Z||000002||Raydar||07-23-2001||09:12 AM||||WOW!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Never heard of this before. Thanks for the heads-up. <BR>Maybe that's why my coupe torque-steers so badly.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||07-23-2001||09:15 AM||||I believe 88s do not have a tie rod end in the rear suspension. Are you sure you are not looking at a pre 88 suspension.  How about pictures to explain. I just looked at a 88 complete rear suspension still on the cradle that I have sitting in the garage to try to understand what you said and could not make sense of it.  (No tie rod end)  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||montage||07-23-2001||09:27 AM||||Skitime I will try to take pictures when I do my brakes hopefully within the week. I know mine is an 88, the tie rods are used to set the toe in on the rear wheels. They are a bit different then the fronts, the bushing goes through the tie rod mounted to the cradle.||||reg||1
Z||000005||skitime||07-23-2001||09:36 AM||||Ok, that is what I thought you might of meant. That part is not called a tie rod but according to the factory manual a fixed adjusting link. The older Fieros have a tie rod and and that is what confused me at first. Here is a picture of the passenger side suspension. I believe you are referring to the suspension link on the right or towards the front of the cradle.  I will definitely check our two 88s out.  I know Fierochic88s car is having similar problems so I will pass on the info.  Thanks<P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7230027.jpg">      <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7230029.jpg">    <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7230030.jpg">    <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7230032.jpg">  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/RearSus.GIF"> <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 07-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||07-23-2001||03:47 PM||||Washer?  Near the edge? This sounds like it could be a poor repair job gone bad...<P>I would get a new 88 cradle immediately (not too0 hard to install, if you just grab the engine with an $200 lift from Pep Boys or such, and remenber to support everything on jack stands twice (car, and engine/tranny even though it's being held from above)<P>or have a shop do it, but GET RID OF THAT CRADLE!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000007||montage||07-23-2001||04:13 PM||||Artherd, this was not a repair job this looks factory to me.<P>Skitime, if you look at your third picture on the left hand side is were the problems start and there might be a crack on the left hand side of the frame mount toward the top (hard to see). Since you have a cradle off could you take a pic of the rear of that same mount (you've take every angle except a rear view this is hard on the car)? ||||reg||1
Z||000008||skitime||07-23-2001||05:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by montage:<BR><B><BR>Skitime, if you look at your third picture on the left hand side is were the problems start and there might be a crack on the left hand side of the frame mount toward the top (hard to see). Since you have a cradle off could you take a pic of the rear of that same mount (you've take every angle except a rear view this is hard on the car)? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7230033.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7230034.jpg"> <BR>This picture is the same location on the drivers side.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7230035.jpg"> <P><P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Toddster||07-23-2001||10:07 PM||||As long as we are on the subject of broken fram parts let me warn you about some that I have seen:<P>Front lower control arm mounts. The bottom of the mount wraps under the front frame member. IF you bottom the car out on a speed bump or something like that youcan shear off these mounts and your lower control arms will be hanging on the upper weld only. The solution is to weld a "skid" plate over the lower welds.<P>I've seen broken engine cradle mounts too. Extra steel helps on the vertical rises of the "U" bracket. Then of course there is everybody's favorite, the Tranny rubber mount tearing in half. I use a piece of steel cable with a little slack in it. One end is mounted to the K frame and the other to the Tranny mount bolt. When the torque twists the engine the slack is taken up and the rubber mount does not have to deal with the stress. The beauty of this set-up versus a solid steel replacement is that you still keep your "low vibration" ride.||||reg||1
